        feel: 'Often the phase of highest energy, mood, libido, and cognitive sharpness for many. Skin tends to look its best. Confidence and sociability often peak.',
        note: 'The follicular phase varies most in length between individuals and cycles. People with longer total cycles usually have longer follicular phases; the luteal phase stays more fixed.'
      },
      ovulation: {
        name: 'Ovulation — around day 14',
        pill: 'p-amber',
        pillLabel: 'Release phase',
        hormones: 'A sharp surge in LH (luteinising hormone), triggered by peak oestrogen, causes the dominant follicle to rupture and release its egg. This is the LH surge that ovulation prediction kits detect.',
        body: 'The egg is released from the ovary and is viable for fertilisation for roughly 12–24 hours. Sperm can survive in the reproductive tract for up to 5 days, so the fertile window stretches from about 5 days before to 1 day after ovulation. Some people feel a one-sided twinge (mittelschmerz). Basal body temperature rises slightly after ovulation and stays elevated through the luteal phase.',
        feel: 'Libido often peaks around this time, an evolutionary cue. Mood, energy, and confidence are often still high. Some experience light spotting or breast tenderness.',
        note: 'In a regular 28-day cycle, ovulation is around day 14. In shorter or longer cycles, it shifts. Ovulation is typically about 14 days before the next period, not 14 days after the last one began.'
      },
      luteal: {
        name: 'Luteal phase — days 15 to 28 (roughly)',
        pill: 'p-purple',
        pillLabel: 'Waiting phase',
        hormones: 'The ruptured follicle becomes the <em>corpus luteum</em>, which produces progesterone (and some oestrogen). Progesterone dominates this phase. If no pregnancy occurs, the corpus luteum regresses around day 26–28 and progesterone collapses, triggering the next period.',
        body: 'Progesterone maintains the thickened uterine lining and prepares it for possible implantation. Basal body temperature stays elevated. Breasts may feel fuller and tender. Bloating is common as progesterone slows gut motility and causes water retention.',
        feel: 'Energy often gradually declines. Mood can become more reactive, anxious, or low in the second half (premenstrual symptoms). Sleep can fragment. Cravings, particularly for carbohydrates and chocolate, are common.',
        note: 'PMS and PMDD symptoms cluster in the second half of this phase. They are caused not by abnormal hormone levels but by individual sensitivity to normal hormonal fluctuations, which is why the same hormones affect people very differently.'
